# Canonical owner CRUD reference

This module is the executable reference for the supported `owner-crud` template. It is intentionally small: title, open/done status, authenticated owner, versioned writes, cursor pagination. It is **not** a universal application generator, offline synchronization protocol, or complete tenant application.

## Composition

Only the server composition root imports `server/register` and calls `register__Module__(context)`. The context is a `ContextBuilder`; construct it with `activeAccountContext` from `core/server/identity`, a server-side active-account lookup, and a request-ID generator. The lookup must bind an immutable authenticated account ID to an active profile, never derive privilege from matching email or request fields. Context is built anew on every request; missing or inactive profiles fail closed.

Add the default `__Module__Page` component to the protected `/__MODULE__` route. Import UI and contracts only on the client. There is no client collection, method simulation, broad publication, or offline queue. The server remains the authorization authority; hiding a UI element is not a permission check.

## Contracts

Every method receives **exactly one** plain-object DTO; all properties listed below are mandatory. Unknown properties are rejected, including `userId`, `ownerId`, roles, tenant IDs and Mongo operators.

| Method | Input | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `__MODULE__.create` | `{ mutationId, title }` | __Module__ DTO |
| `__MODULE__.get` | `{ id }` | __Module__ DTO or `not-found` |
| `__MODULE__.list` | `{ after: string or null, limit: 1..100 }` | `{ items, next: string or null }` |
| `__MODULE__.update` | `{ id, version, title, status: "open" or "done" }` | Current __Module__ DTO |
| `__MODULE__.remove` | `{ id, version }` | `{ deleted: true }` |

A __Module__ DTO contains exactly `id`, `title`, `status`, `version`, `createdAt`, and `updatedAt`. Titles are trimmed and must have 1–200 characters. Times are server-issued epoch milliseconds. The initial version is 1. Owner IDs and tenant scope never leave the server. There are no field defaults silently accepting omitted mutation properties.

Owner scope includes both authenticated owner ID and `tenantId: null`; tenant documents cannot be reached via owner operations. The generic tenant policy in `core/server/policy` requires a fresh server membership lookup and explicit allowed roles. It denies missing/revoked membership, wrong user, wrong tenant and disallowed roles. This reference does not expose tenant endpoints; a tenant module requires its own repository wiring, provisioning, revocation/concurrency policy and integration suite.

## Concurrency and errors

Mongo updates and deletions include owner scope AND expected version in the atomic selector. Concurrent changes from the same base version have at most one winner. Deletion records a tombstone rather than physically removing the record. No-match writes return `conflict` for both stale and inaccessible records, avoiding an existence oracle. Reload before retrying; do not blindly retry writes. The update response is a scoped read of the current record after the atomic mutation, so a subsequent concurrent mutation may already be reflected in it. If that record was deleted concurrently, the update returns `not-found` even though its mutation may have committed. Create uses a required client-generated mutation ID retained across transport retries. Its primary key is a SHA-256 hash of the owner and mutation ID; the database primary-key constraint arbitrates concurrent retries. A duplicate with the same normalized creation title returns the same current scoped record, while a different creation payload returns `conflict`. The mutation ID, original creation title and deletion marker remain server-only. Tombstones retain the key so delayed create replay returns `not-found`, never resurrects a deleted __MODULE__. Do not purge tombstones without defining a bounded deduplication retention protocol. Update/delete retries remain version-CAS safe but may return `conflict` after a lost successful response; reload to reconcile. This is not an exactly-once delivery protocol.

Pagination uses exclusive `_id` order with an extra-row probe. `next` is null on the final page. This is not a snapshot; concurrent inserts/deletes can change later pages. Single-document writes do not need transactions. The optional `core/server/transaction` adapter binds supplied repositories to one caller-provided Mongo session and propagates errors, with no unsafe non-transaction fallback. Multi-document users must supply session-aware repositories and run replica-set rollback tests; the reference does not claim those unexecuted integration guarantees.

Output contracts also validate on the server, and projection is explicit. Operational failures are returned as generic `internal-error`, without database internals. Rate limits are per connection/operation and per authenticated identity/operation in the current Meteor process; multi-replica abuse protection needs deployment-level coordination.

## Verification

- `tests/core.node.test.ts`: deterministic contract, context-shift, scope, tenant-membership, projection, CAS behavior, pagination and transaction-port tests. Its in-memory CAS is an executable model, not evidence of Mongo atomicity.
- `tests/policy.test.ts`: server-only Meteor test import. Exercises registered wrappers, real Mongo owner/CAS behavior and a real DDP connection rejecting anonymous/forged inputs. Fixtures use a test-only injected identity resolver, not production login provisioning.
- UI implements logged-out, loading, empty, error, validation, editing, delete confirmation, conflict feedback and pagination states. Pending responses are discarded after account changes. No E2E or accessibility success is implied merely by these states existing.

Do not import `tests/policy.test.ts` in a production entrypoint. It creates test-only methods and fixture identities.
